---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import FeatureItem from "../components/FeatureItem.astro";
import { REPO_URL, LICENSES } from "../lib/config.ts";
---
<Base title="About" description="Why OpenDeviceDB exists, who runs it and how it is licensed.">
  <div class="article">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      OpenDeviceDB is an independent, open directory of devices whose protocols or firmware have been
      reverse engineered — drones, cameras, robots, wearables and everything else whose vendor stopped caring.
    </p>
    <img class="about-art" src="/images/about.webp" alt="A central database connected to a drone, a camera, a phone and a router" width="1200" height="900" />

    <h2>Why</h2>
    <p>
      AI agents made reverse engineering cheap. That is good news for anyone holding a drone, a camera
      or a vacuum robot that only talks to a dead cloud. It also means thousands of people now
      re-derive the same protocol for the same device, in parallel, and keep the result on a private disk.
    </p>
    <p>
      Community databases already answer "which free firmware runs on this model" — for routers, Zigbee
      devices, smart plugs, IP cameras — each in its own category. None answers, across categories:
      has this device been done, where is the result, is someone working on it right now, and was it
      verified on real hardware? OpenDeviceDB answers that, for humans on this website and for agents
      through an <a href="/agents/">MCP server</a>, and credits the <a href="/sources/">sources</a> it aggregates.
    </p>

    <div class="card glow principles">
      <h2>Principles</h2>
      <FeatureItem icon="file" title="Files in git are the database">One YAML file per device, changed by pull request. This site is a static build of the repository; there is no server-side database.</FeatureItem>
      <FeatureItem icon="database" title="Aggregate, don't duplicate">Imported records link back to their source project.</FeatureItem>
      <FeatureItem icon="user-check" title="Claims prevent duplicate work">Say you are on a device; claims expire.</FeatureItem>
      <FeatureItem icon="ai-editing" title="AI output is not trusted by default">Confidence per interface; "verified" means a hardware replay.</FeatureItem>
      <FeatureItem icon="shield" title="Legally boring">Descriptions, original code and links only. See the <a href="/policy/">policy</a>.</FeatureItem>
    </div>

    <h2>Independent and open</h2>
    <p>No vendor affiliation, no accounts, no tracking, no ads.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Code: <strong>{LICENSES.code}</strong> — a hosted fork must publish its changes.</li>
      <li>Data (<code>data/</code>, <code>news/</code>): <strong>{LICENSES.data}</strong>. Imported snapshots keep the license of their upstream source.</li>
      <li>Agent skill file (<code>packages/mcp/SKILL.md</code>): <strong>{LICENSES.skill}</strong>, so other tools can embed it.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Source, issues and discussions: <a href={REPO_URL}>{REPO_URL.replace("https://", "")}</a>. This is a prototype; the format and the policy are open for discussion.</p>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .about-art { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: var(--max-narrow); height: auto; margin: 0 auto var(--space-section-gap); }
  .principles { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 24px; margin: 2.2em 0; padding: 32px; }
  .principles h2 { margin: 0; }
</style>
